# Vercel Web Analytics Implementation

## Summary
Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics for the Next.js App Router project.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/analytics` package using npm
- Updated `package.json` to include `@vercel/analytics` as a dependency
- Updated `package-lock.json` with the new dependency tree

### 2. Root Layout Update
**File Modified:** `frontend/src/app/root.layout.tsx`

Added the Analytics component to the root layout file:
- Imported `Analytics` from `@vercel/analytics/next`
- Added `<Analytics />` component inside the `<body>` tag, after `<SpeedInsights />` and other components
- Placement follows best practices: inside body tag after children and other UI components

### 3. Implementation Details
The project structure:
- Uses Next.js App Router (app directory)
- Root layout located at `src/app/root.layout.tsx` (re-exported from `src/app/layout.tsx`)
- Already had Vercel Speed Insights configured, so Analytics was added alongside it

### 4. Verification
- ✅ Build completed successfully (`npm run build`)
- ✅ All dependencies installed without conflicts
- ✅ TypeScript compilation passed
- ✅ No runtime errors or warnings
- ✅ Lock files properly updated

### 5. Files Staged for Commit
- `package.json` - Added @vercel/analytics dependency
- `package-lock.json` - Updated dependency lock file
- `src/app/root.layout.tsx` - Added Analytics component

## Next Steps
The Analytics component will automatically start tracking page views and web vitals once deployed to Vercel. No additional configuration is required unless you want to customize the analytics behavior with specific options.

## Notes
- The Analytics component works seamlessly with the existing SpeedInsights component
- Both components are designed to have minimal impact on page performance
- Analytics data will be available in the Vercel dashboard once the application is deployed
